static String f(Object o) {
    return switch (o) {
        case null -> "null!";
        case Integer i when i > 10 -> "big " + i;
        case Integer i -> "small " + i;
        case String s when s.isEmpty() -> "empty";
        case String s -> s.toUpperCase();
        case int[] a -> "ints " + a.length;
        default -> "other";
    };
}
f(null); f(5); f(50); f(""); f("q"); f(new int[2]); f(1.0);
The run printed null!, small 5, big 50, empty, Q, ints 2, other. Cases are tested from the top, and a guard's failure moves on to the next case. Without case null, the switch throws NullPointerException. Swapping the two Integer cases is a compile error, because the unguarded one dominates.

var sl = new ArrayList<>(List.of(1, 2, 3));
var rev = sl.reversed();
sl.add(4);
rev.addFirst(9);
System.out.println(sl + " " + rev);
It prints [1, 2, 3, 4, 9] [9, 4, 3, 2, 1]. reversed() is a live view: sl.add(4) appears in it, and addFirst on the reverse view adds at the end of the original.

Integer a = 127, b = 127, c = 128, d = 128;
System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (c == d) + " " + c.equals(d));
It prints true false true. Autoboxing calls Integer.valueOf, which caches -128 to 127, so a and b are the same object. 128 isn't cached by default, so c == d compares two different objects. Always compare wrappers with equals. A second autoboxing trap: unboxing a null Integer throws NullPointerException.

Java 7. Any AutoCloseable declared in the header is closed automatically, in reverse order of declaration, whether the body completes or throws. If the body throws and close() also throws, the body's exception is the one propagated, and the close failures are attached to it as suppressed exceptions, rather than hiding it as a finally block would.
try (Res r1 = new Res("1"); Res r2 = new Res("2")) {   // each close() throws
    throw new RuntimeException("body");
} catch (RuntimeException e) {
    System.out.println(e.getMessage() + " suppressed=" + Arrays.toString(e.getSuppressed()));
}
// close 2
// close 1
// body suppressed=[java.lang.IllegalStateException: close 2, java.lang.IllegalStateException: close 1]
Since Java 9 the header can also name an existing effectively final variable: try (r) { ... }.

| Switch statement | Switch expression (14) | Pattern switch (21)
Produces a value | No | Yes (yield from a block) | Either form
Case labels | Constants: int-like, enum (5), String (7) | Same constants, several per label | Type patterns, record patterns, guards with when, null
Fall-through | Yes with :, a classic bug | None with -> | None with ->
Exhaustive | Not required | Required | Required, even as a statement
Null selector | NullPointerException | NullPointerException | Handled if there's case null
Case order | Doesn't matter | Doesn't matter | Matters: first match wins; dominated cases are compile errors
String old;
switch (day) { case 1: case 7: old = "weekend"; break; default: old = "weekday"; }
String expr = switch (day) { case 1, 7 -> "weekend"; default -> "weekday"; };
String pat = switch (o) {
    case Integer n when n > 10 -> "big";
    case Integer n -> "small";
    case null -> "null";
    default -> "other";
};
Rule of thumb: use arrow-form switch expressions for constant cases, and a pattern switch over a sealed hierarchy instead of instanceof chains. Keep the colon form only in old code.

| Anonymous class (1.1) | Lambda (8) | Method reference (8)
Target | Any interface or class, any number of methods | Functional interface only | Functional interface only
Meaning of this | The anonymous object | The enclosing instance | Not applicable
Own state | Can have fields | No fields; captures effectively final locals | Bound receiver, if any
Scope | New scope: may shadow outer names | Same scope as the enclosing code: can't redeclare a local | Not applicable
Compiled to | A separate class file (Outer$1) | invokedynamic; class created at run time | Same as lambda
Runnable anon = new Runnable() {
    String name = "anon";
    @Override public void run() { System.out.println(this.name + " " + Cmp.this.name); }
};
Runnable lambda = () -> System.out.println(this.name);
anon.run();     // anon outer
lambda.run();   // outer
Rule of thumb: prefer a method reference when one exists and reads clearly, a lambda for short logic, and an anonymous class only when you need state, several methods, or an abstract class.
